course: dictionary
lesson: 15666
title: Thoroughly: carefully and completely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Thoroughly is an adverb, a word that tells you how something is done or how strong a feeling is. It has two main meanings: carefully and completely, so nothing is missed, or very much and totally.
explain: «Wash your hands thoroughly before you eat.» Here thoroughly means carefully and completely, so every part of your hands gets clean. A quick rinse is not enough. Try it: put "carefully and completely" in place of thoroughly, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «I thoroughly enjoyed the trip to the zoo.» Here thoroughly means very much. It makes the feeling stronger, like "really" or "totally". Ask yourself: did the child enjoy it a lot? If yes, thoroughly means very much.
fact: Thoroughly is an adverb: it tells you how someone does something, or how strong a feeling is.
fact: «Thoroughly» means carefully and completely, so nothing is missed: «Check your work thoroughly.»
fact: «Thoroughly» also means very much or totally: «I thoroughly enjoyed the film.»
fact: In safety and health, thoroughly matters: «Cook chicken thoroughly» so that every part is cooked and safe to eat.
fact: In everyday talk, thoroughly adds strength, as in «thoroughly fed up», which means very fed up.
exercise: «Wash your hands thoroughly before lunch.» What does thoroughly mean here? | Only a little | Very loudly | *Carefully and completely, so no dirt is missed | Quickly, with a splash of water
exercise: «I thoroughly enjoyed the party.» What does thoroughly mean here? | Not at all | Sadly | Carefully | *Very much
exercise: In «Ella checked her spelling thoroughly», what does thoroughly tell us? | When the test was | What colour her pencil was | Who checked the spelling | *How Ella checked, carefully and completely
test: «Cook the chicken thoroughly so it is safe to eat.» Why must it be cooked thoroughly? | So it tastes sour | So it looks pretty on the plate | *So every part is cooked and safe to eat | So it cooks faster than usual
test: Which sentence uses thoroughly to mean very much? | *«We thoroughly loved the museum.» | «Please read the rules thoroughly.» | «Dry the dishes thoroughly.»
exam: Jayden writes, «I thoroughly checked my maths answers.» Does thoroughly mean very much, or carefully and completely, here? | Very much, because he checked a lot | Totally wrong, because checking is never useful | Quickly and loudly | *Carefully and completely, so no mistake was missed
